blocks.en:
Language modeling is essentially an n n -gram estimation task. Recall that the n n -gram estimation amounts to estimating the conditional probability tensor p ( x n ∣ x 1 : n − 1 ) p(x_{n}\mid x_{1:n-1}) .
The frequentist method quickly becomes infeasible due to an exponential dependence on sample complexity and memory requirements.
However, numerous empirical studies indicate that this tensor is extremely sparse, which gives rise to different algorithms.
Smoothing re-distributes the probability mass through a linear interpolation ( Chen and Goodman, 1996 ) ;
PLRE takes advantages of conditional factorization into lower-rank tensors ( Parikh et al., 2014a ) ;
Some algorithms use backoff ( Goodman, 2001 ; Nakamura and Shikano, 1989 ) or scales by brute force ( Brants et al., 2007 ) up to the unbounded suffix-array construction of infini-gram ( Liu et al., 2025 )

